---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
---

<DocsLayout title="Data Format">
  <p>
    A graph is a JSON object with four arrays. Concatenate numeric records into
    flat arrays; string indexes and port indexes start at zero.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Array</th>
        <th>Record</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <code>strings</code>
        </td>
        <td>Labels and CSS color strings</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <code>nodes</code>
        </td>
        <td>
          <code>id, labelIndex, colorIndex</code>
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <code>edges</code>
        </td>
        <td>
          <code>sourceId, sourcePort, targetId, targetPort</code>
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <code>subgraphs</code>
        </td>
        <td>
          <code>nodeId, inId, outId</code> (owner, entry, exit)
        </td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <pre><code>&#123;
  "strings": ["A", "#6aa0ff", "B"],
  "nodes": [0, 0, 1, 1, 2, 1],
  "edges": [0, 0, 1, 0],
  "subgraphs": []
&#125;</code></pre>

  <h2>Requirements</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Arrays may be empty. Numeric array lengths must be multiples of their
      record widths: 3 for nodes and subgraphs, 4 for edges.
    </li>
    <li>
      IDs must be safe integers; node IDs must be unique. No record sorting is
      required. All referenced node IDs and string indexes must exist.
    </li>
    <li>
      Every string-table entry must be a string. Label and color indexes must be
      integers; ports must be integers with{" "}
      <code>0 &lt;= port &lt; 4,294,967,295</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      Edges connect output ports to input ports and require{" "}
      <code>sourceId &lt; targetId</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      Each subgraph requires <code>inId &lt; outId</code>, its owner outside{" "}
      <code>[inId, outId]</code>, and a unique <code>(inId, outId)</code> pair.
      Individual boundary IDs may be shared. Boundaries cannot own subgraphs;
      ownership must form an acyclic hierarchy rooted at the top level.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Scopes and ports</h2>
  <p>
    An ordinary node belongs to the narrowest open interval{" "}
    <code>inId &lt; id &lt; outId</code>; equal widths favor the greater{" "}
    <code>inId</code>. Nodes outside all intervals belong to the top level.
    Entry and exit nodes aren't ordinary members; they render as frame
    boundaries, not node bodies.
  </p>
  <p>
    Edges stay within one scope: ordinary node to ordinary node, entry to node,
    node to exit, or entry to exit. Empty scopes allow entry-to-exit
    passthroughs. An ordinary endpoint determines the scope when boundaries are
    shared; boundary-only edges require that scope's entry/exit pair. The
    owner's ports connect in its own scope; child boundary ports connect in the
    child scope.
  </p>
  <p>
    Each node's input/output count is its highest referenced input/output port
    index plus one, or zero when unused. Counts include all uses of a shared
    boundary ID. Edge order determines edge identity and color; subgraph order
    determines frame order within an owner.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
